Return a real process when asked to start an empty batch file.
spawnve() can't run an empty batch file, so spawnveq() pretended that
it had and returned 0. That is right for P_WAIT, where it is the exit
status, but for P_NOWAIT it is a process handle: mingw_spawn() turned
it into pid 0 and the wait for it failed. time printed "wait: Invalid
argument" and a report of uninitialised values, xargs and find -exec
reported the command as failed, and sh read the exit code of a null
handle and reported garbage such as "Error 0x7fff" and status 255.

For P_NOWAIT, run "%COMSPEC% /d /c rem", which does nothing, in place
of the batch file, so the caller gets a real process that exits with 0.
Without COMSPEC, fail with ENOEXEC rather than return a bogus handle.

---
 win32/process.c |   14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/win32/process.c
+++ b/win32/process.c
@@ -196,9 +196,21 @@
 
 	if (has_bat_suffix(path)) {
 		/* Another special case:  spawnve returns ENOEXEC when passed an
-		 * empty batch file.  Pretend it worked. */
+		 * empty batch file.  Pretend it worked.  A caller that waits
+		 * for the process needs a real one, so run a command that does
+		 * nothing in its place. */
 		if (st.st_size == 0) {
+			char *rem_argv[] = {
+				(char *)"cmd", (char *)"/d", (char *)"/c", (char *)"rem",
+				NULL
+			};
+			const char *comspec = getenv("COMSPEC");
+
 			ret = 0;
+			if (mode == P_NOWAIT) {
+				errno = ENOEXEC;
+				ret = comspec ? spawnve(mode, comspec, rem_argv, env) : -1;
+			}
 			goto done;
 		}
 	}
